          {
            "end": 1449,
            "passage_index": 1,
            "quote": "dasatinib (5 mg/kg/day) or vehicle by gavage or zoledronic acid (ZOL; 100 microg/kg/6 weeks) subcutaneously. Following 4, 8, and 12 weeks of treatment, serum biochemical, bone morphometric, and histologic analyses were performed. Whole-body bone mineral density and tibial cortical thickness where unchanged in the dasatinib- or ZOL-treated animals relative to controls. However, micro-computed tomographic (microCT) analysis of cancellous bone at the proximal tibias showed that trabecular volume (BV/TV) and thickness (Tb.Th) were increased in dasatinib-treated animals at levels comparable with those of the ZOL-treated group. These changes were associated with a decrease in osteoclast numbers (N.Oc/B.Pm) and surface (Oc.S/BS) and decreased serum levels of the osteoclast marker c-terminal collagen crosslinks (CTX-1).",
